   225	Fig. 3. Workflow of Our Approach
   226	
   227	
   228	
   229	This example seems straightforward to reproduce. However, it may cause significant challenges to
   230	existing automated reproduction techniques.
   231	To reproduce a given bug report, the existing approaches, ReCDroid [ 52 ], Yakusu [ 24 ], and
   232	ReproBot [ 50 ], employ two stages. In the first stage, they extract the entities for each step from
   233	the text using natural language processing ( NLP ) techniques. As illustrated in Figure 1b, these step
   234	entities capture the essential information regarding the UI action expressed by a step. They include
   235	the type of the UI action ( 푎푐푡푖표푛 ), the description of the action’s target widget ( 푡푎푟푔푒푡 ) etc. Then, in
   236	the second stage, these approaches dynamically explore the app and search for a path of UI events
   237	that match the steps.
   238	Assuming the existing approaches can perfectly parse the step entities, their search algorithms
   239	still face limitations that hinder their reproduction performance. When searching for the UI events
   240	that match the steps, ReCDroid and Yakusu employ a greedy strategy, meaning that they will
   241	always prioritize to match a given step with the most similar UI event on the current UI screen.
   242	However, such a greedy strategy could lead them to choose an incorrect UI event when the next step
   243	is missing or inaccurately described. When reproducing the example bug report, both approaches
   244	would face problems finding the first event 푒 1 in Figure 2, as the corresponding step 푠 푥 is not
   245	included in the bug report. Consequently, both approaches need to exhaustively explore the UI
   246	trying to find the matched UI event on the following pages. Even if they could correctly find event
   247	푒 1, they would face another challenge to correctly match step 푠 2 with event 푒 4 . When reaching
   248	screen three in Figure 2, both approaches prematurely match step 푠 2 to an incorrect event 푒 5 (i.e.,
   249	click “Add a medicine” button) since they are unaware of the missing step 푠 푦 . This eventually cause
   250	their failure in reproducing this bug report. The same problems also happen to ReproBot. To find
   251	the UI events matching the steps, ReproBot leverages a RL algorithm to guide the dynamic search
   252	in the app. Specifically, during the search process, the RL algorithm guides ReproBot to randomly
   253	evaluate matches between a step and a less-similar UI event, attempting to find a better-matched
   254	event on the following UI s. ReproBot also leverages such a non-deterministic mechanism to
   255	reveal the potential missing steps in the bug report. However, as one of the limitations discussed
   256	in ReproBot’s paper [ 50 ], when reproducing the example bug report, ReproBot needs to try
   257	different possible matches, which is time-consuming and cannot guarantee finding the correctly
   258	matched UI events.
   259	
   260	The limitations of existing works motivated us to design a better approach to improve the second
   261	stage of the reproduction process, i.e., the search for the UI events to reproduce the steps. We
   262	introduce the details of our approach in the next section.
