     1	# Roam: Mobile Bug Report Reproduction via Global Search on the App UI Model.
     2	Roam is a technique to reproduce mobile bug report leveraging an existing app's UI model. In this artifact, we provided the sources code of Roam, evaluation dataset, and detailed documentations to reuse and validate Roam. The badges that we claim are documented in **STATUS.md** file. The accepted paper is also included in "paper.pdf".
     3	
     4	This README file mainly covers the running configuration of Roam, dataset details and steps to run Roam on the dataset. Before continue reading this file, please first refer to **REQUIREMENTS.md** file to setup the running environment for Roam. Then, please refer to **INSTALLS.md** file to test out Roam on an example following the tutorial. 
     5	
     6	## Roam's Configuration
     7	### Input Arguments
     8	Here are the commonly-used input arguments supported by Roam. For the whole list of the arguements, please use `--help` to check it.
     9	* --appId {XX}: The id of the subject.
    10	* --apkPath {XX}: The path to the apk file of the app under test.
    11	* --outputDir {XX}: The directory where Roam can generate output files.
    12	* --s2rsPath {XX}: A text file containing the step entities of the bug report.
    13	* --crashMsgPath {XX}: A file contains the error message that will be triggered when reproducing a crash bug report. Roam will use this information to determine when to stop. For non-crash bug reports, do not specify this argument.
    14	* -nonCrashReport: Add this flag when you are running Roam on a non-crash bug report.
    15	* --utgPath {xxx}: Specify the path of the folder containing the pre-built UTG files.
    16	* -createNewEmulator: When adding this parameter, Roam will create a new Android emulator. If not specified, Roam will try to reuse existing Android emulators on your local machine. If you want Roam to use your local emulators, please name your emulator as the same with line 27 in [emulator_helper.py](./ReproductionTool/reproduction_utils/emulator_helper.py). We recommend you to add this argument when running Roam in docker container since there is not pre-installed emulators.
    17	
    18	### Output Structure
    19	The structure of Roam output is shown as below:
    20	* match_result: This folder stores the computed best step-event matches and reproduction UI event paths.
    21	* reproduction_result: This folder stores the screenshots, UI view hierarchy files and emulator device logs while Roam executing each UI event path on the device (under the "screenshots" sub-folder). If Roam successfully reproduce a crash bug report, a file "success_events.json" will be created under this folder, which shows the UI event path that reproduce the bug.
    22	
    23	## Run Roam on Evaluation Dataset
    24	In this section, we introduce how to run Roam on other subjects from the evaluation dataset. In the following, we first introduce the dataset and related files. Then introduce how you can run Roam on a subject.
    25	
    26	### Evaluation Dataset
    27	The dataset contains 72 subjects (44 crash reports and 28 non-crash reports). The details of each subject is shown in "Evaluation/dataset.csv". Specifically, the csv file includes the subject id, bug report link and Android SDK version, etc. 
    28	
    29	The related files for each subject are under "Evaluation/dataset" folder. Here is the structure of the dataset:
    30	* "apks": This folder contains the apk file for each subject.
    31	* "crash_msgs": This folder contains the target error message (in each text file) for each crash bug report.
    32	* "neo4j" and "utgs": These two folders stores the pre-built UTG of each subject. The UTGs are partially stored in the Neo4J graph database and partially in local files. The "neo4j" folder stores the files required by the Neo4J database. And the "utgs" stores the other part.
    33	* "setup_scripts": This folder stores the Android UIAutomation scripts for each subject, which provides necessary setup steps for the reproduction (such as log in an account).
    34	* "s2rs": This folder contains the step entities for each subject.
    35	
    36	### Run Roam on a Subject
    37	To run Roam on a specific subject, please follow the steps below:
    38	
    39	1. Find the id of the subject you want to run from the [dataset sheet](./Evaluation/dataset.csv).
    40	2. Find the corresponding apk file, step entities file, and crash message file (if it's a crash report) in the dataset folder. You should be able to locate them using the subject id. Copy them to the "test_input" folder under "ReproductionTool". Rename them to be the same as the example ones.
    41	3. Find the prebuilt UTG files under the "Evaluation/dataset/utgs" folder. Also replace the corresponding files under "ReproductionTool/test_input/gui_model".
    42	4. Find the corresponding setup script file. Copy it to "ReproductionTool/reproducer/setup_run.py".
    43	5. Start the running environment following the "REQUIREMENTS" file
    44	6. Follow the tutorial in "INSTALL.md" to run Roam. Remember to use the app id and corresponding Android version of the chosen subject when passing the arguments.
    45	
    46	### Verify the Experiment Results
    47	The detailed results used in the paper is provided in "Evaluation/results.pdf". There are three metrics used in the paper: match accuracy, reproduction result and running time. Here, we provide the steps to compute these results from the running results of our tool.
    48	
    49	* Match Accuracy: The match accuracy is measured by comparing the generated matches in the "match_result/matches" folder of the output files with the ground truth matches. The ground truth matches are under "Evaluation/ground_truth_matches".
    50	
    51	* Reproduction Result: For a crash bug report, if the tool successfully reproduced it, there will be a "success_events.json" file generated in the output. For a non-crash bug report, we manually verified the reproduction results by inspecting the screenshots under "reproduction_results/screenshots" folder in the output files.
    52	
    53	* Running time: To get the running time, please can check the timestamps in the log file in the output files.


